      "regionalNodes": "Regional nodes are the perigastric nodes along the lesser and greater curvatures and the nodes along the left gastric, common hepatic, splenic, and celiac arteries, plus hepatoduodenal nodes. Involvement of other intra-abdominal nodes (for example retropancreatic, para-aortic, portal, mesenteric) is classified as distant metastasis (M1). recommends removal and examination of at least 16 regional nodes (30 or more is preferred) for reliable pN staging.",
      "histologicGrade": "Adenocarcinoma is graded G1 (well differentiated) to G3 (poorly differentiated), with G4/undifferentiated in some schemes; GX when grade cannot be assessed. The Lauren classification (intestinal vs diffuse vs mixed) is also commonly reported and carries prognostic weight.",
      "histopathologicTypes": "The great majority are adenocarcinomas, subclassified by Lauren type into intestinal, diffuse, and mixed forms. Signet-ring cell carcinoma (a diffuse-type variant), mucinous, tubular, papillary, and poorly cohesive carcinomas are recognized WHO subtypes. Rare non-adenocarcinoma tumors (lymphoma, GIST, neuroendocrine) use separate staging systems.",
      "prognosticFactors": [
        "Depth of invasion (T category)",
        "Number of involved regional nodes (N category) and total nodes examined",
        "Distant metastasis including peritoneal spread (M category)",
        "Resection margin status (R0 vs R1/R2)",
        "Lauren classification (intestinal vs diffuse)",
        "Histologic grade",
        "HER2 status",
        "Location including esophagogastric junction involvement",
        "Lymphovascular invasion"
      ]
    },
